import Native

{-
   `moveMemory` again, one tier up.

   # extensions: v3

   Everything this asserts is `MoveMemory.yana`'s, and the file is that one with this comment in
   front of it. What makes it a second fixture is the **width**, and the reason it is a fixture at
   all is that a directive is the only way to get one: `test/directives.h` compiles every fixture
   that names no level at **v2**, so the whole suite - this one included - only ever exercised a
   sixteen-byte register.

   That is not a gap in coverage so much as a gap in what the coverage *meant*. Every boundary in
   `moveMemory` is stated in widths: the ladder's rungs are `n` transfers of `w` bytes covering
   `[nw, 2nw)`, and which of them is reachable is a function of the register. At v2 the vector rung
   starts at sixteen and meets the word rung below it exactly; at v3 it starts at thirty-two and
   leaves `[16, 32)` for a word rung that reached only sixteen - so **counts of seventeen to
   thirty-one wrote every byte except the eight in the middle**, in a build nothing here ever made.
   This file is what makes that build exist.

   v4 would want the same again for `[32, 64)`, and the rung is written; it cannot be a fixture on
   a host without AVX-512, which is what would have to run it.
-}

{-
   `moveMemory` - Analysis-Library.md part 7, finding 1.

   The overlapping block move, which `copyMemory` deliberately is not: the primitive says nothing
   about the order it touches bytes in, so a caller whose regions overlap - `Array.remove` closing
   the gap it just made - had no operation to reach for and was relying on the lowering ascending.

   What this asserts is the property that makes it a *move* rather than a copy: every byte of the
   source is read before the write that could reach it. That is invisible in a golden diff, since a
   move that clobbered its own source and one that did not are the same instructions, so all of it is
   read back out of the buffer afterwards.

   Four overlaps and two degenerate cases, named one at a time so that a failure says which shape
   broke. The two directions are separate tests because they are separate loops with the opposite
   argument, and each is run at two lengths: one short enough that only the byte tail executes, and
   one long enough that the wide step does the work and hands a partial tail to the loop below it. A
   one-byte gap is the worst case for both - it is the distance at which every wide step writes over
   bytes it has already read - and it is the distance `Array.remove` has for a byte array.

   **And then `sweep`, which is the one that actually holds the implementation down.** The body is a
   vector loop with an overlapping ragged end, so the shapes that break it are the ones where a
   length or a gap sits just either side of the register's width - and which width that is depends on
   the level the target was built for. Naming lengths by hand cannot cover that, so the sweep walks
   every length to 200 and every gap to 96 in both directions, which crosses sixteen, thirty-two and
   sixty-four several times over - and `sweepWide` below walks a coarser cross product past it, for
   the four-register group the dense one never runs twice. It checks three things per move rather than one: the moved bytes,
   the bytes in front of the destination, and the bytes behind it. The last two are what catch a
   store that ran wide - a tail transfer placed at `n - W` is only correct because it *stops* at `n`,
   and nothing about the moved region itself would say if it did not.
-}

-- The buffer, filled with `0, 1, 2, ...` so that any byte's correct value is its own index and a
-- clobber shows up as a repeat.
fn fill(buffer: %U8, count: I64) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: I64
    while i < count:
        store(buffer + i, truncate(i) :: U8)
        i = i + 1

-- Whether `count` bytes from `at` are the ascending run starting at `first`, which is what every
-- move below leaves behind. The comparison truncates because `fill` does: the sweep reaches past
-- index 255 and the run wraps there, so an `I64` on the right would fail on the buffer rather than
-- on the move.
fn runs(buffer: %U8, at: I64, first: I64, count: I64) -> Bool:
    let &i = 0 :: I64
    while i < count:
        if (*(buffer + at + i)) != (truncate(first + i) :: U8) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   Downwards, which is `Array.remove`'s direction: the destination is below the source, so the loop
   has to ascend.

   Two lengths and two gaps. 15 bytes at a gap of one runs the wide step once and leaves seven for
   the tail; 37 at a gap of three runs it four times and leaves five. Both are the case where the
   write of one step lands on top of bytes the previous step read.
-}
fn descending() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(64)

    fill(buffer, 16)
    moveMemory(buffer, buffer + 1, 15)
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 1, 15))

    fill(buffer, 40)
    moveMemory(buffer, buffer + 3, 37)
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 3, 37))

    -- A gap wide enough that the regions never met, which is the one path that is still the block
    -- operation. It has to answer the same thing.
    fill(buffer, 64)
    moveMemory(buffer, buffer + 32, 32)
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 32, 32))

    freeHeap(buffer)
    return

{-
   And upwards, where the same statement holds with the loop descending.

   The bytes below the destination are left alone, which is checked as well: a loop that ran the
   wrong way would leave the source's own prefix rewritten, and only reading it back says so.
-}
fn ascending() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(64)

    fill(buffer, 16)
    moveMemory(buffer + 1, buffer, 15)
    assert(runs(buffer, 1, 0, 15))
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 0, 1))

    fill(buffer, 40)
    moveMemory(buffer + 3, buffer, 37)
    assert(runs(buffer, 3, 0, 37))
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 0, 3))

    fill(buffer, 64)
    moveMemory(buffer + 32, buffer, 32)
    assert(runs(buffer, 32, 0, 32))

    freeHeap(buffer)
    return

-- The two arguments that do nothing, stated because both are reachable from `Array.remove`: removing
-- the last element leaves nothing to move, and a zero count is what that arrives as.
fn degenerate() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(64)

    fill(buffer, 16)
    moveMemory(buffer, buffer + 1, 0)
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 0, 16))

    moveMemory(buffer, buffer, 16)
    assert(runs(buffer, 0, 0, 16))

    freeHeap(buffer)
    return

{-
   Every length and every gap, in both directions, checked at all three edges.

   `fill` covers eight bytes past the far end of whichever region is higher, so that "behind the
   destination" is a real question with an ascending answer rather than whatever the allocator left.
   The moves themselves are the whole cross product, disjoint pairs included: a gap at or above the
   length is the `copyMemory` path and has to answer the same thing as the two that overlap.
-}
fn sweep() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(512)
    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &count = 0 :: I64

    while count <= 200:
        let &gap = 1 :: I64

        while gap <= 96:
            -- The destination below the source. `[0, count)` becomes the run starting at `gap`, and
            -- nothing at or above `count` may have been touched.
            fill(buffer, gap + count + 8)
            moveMemory(buffer, buffer + gap, count)
            if !runs(buffer, 0, gap, count) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, count, count, 8) then bad = bad + 1

            -- And above it. Now it is the prefix below `gap` that has to be intact, and the eight
            -- bytes past `gap + count` on the other side.
            fill(buffer, gap + count + 8)
            moveMemory(buffer + gap, buffer, count)
            if !runs(buffer, gap, 0, count) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, 0, 0, gap) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, gap + count, gap + count, 8) then bad = bad + 1

            gap = gap + 1

        count = count + 1

    freeHeap(buffer)
    assert(bad == 0)
    return

{-
   And the same cross product again over the lengths the *unrolled* group reaches, which the sweep
   above stops short of.

   The loop moves four registers a turn, so the group is sixty-four bytes at v2, a hundred and
   twenty-eight at v3 and two hundred and fifty-six at v4 - and above two hundred bytes the sweep
   above never runs one twice, never leaves a remainder for the one-register loop behind it, and
   never sees a gap narrower than a whole group. All three are where a four-wide move differs from a
   one-wide one, and all three are here.

   Coarse on purpose. What the dense sweep is for is the rungs and the ragged end, where every
   single length is its own case; what this is for is the group, where what matters is the length's
   residue against sixty-four, a hundred and twenty-eight and two hundred and fifty-six and the
   gap's size against the same. A step of three and a step of five walk every one of those residues
   several times over in a few thousand moves rather than a hundred thousand.
-}
fn sweepWide() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(1024)
    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &count = 128 :: I64

    while count <= 640:
        let &gap = 1 :: I64

        while gap <= 200:
            fill(buffer, gap + count + 8)
            moveMemory(buffer, buffer + gap, count)
            if !runs(buffer, 0, gap, count) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, count, count, 8) then bad = bad + 1

            fill(buffer, gap + count + 8)
            moveMemory(buffer + gap, buffer, count)
            if !runs(buffer, gap, 0, count) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, 0, 0, gap) then bad = bad + 1
            if !runs(buffer, gap + count, gap + count, 8) then bad = bad + 1

            gap = gap + 5

        count = count + 3

    freeHeap(buffer)
    assert(bad == 0)
    return

fn main() -> Int:
    descending()
    ascending()
    degenerate()
    sweep()
    sweepWide()
    return 0
